The Record {#chapter-record}
=====================================

## Definition ## {#section-record-definition}

Every <dfn>Record</dfn> has the meaning defined in [[ISO15489]] and MUST consist of exactly four components:

1. [=Identity=]: the DID of the Record ([Record Identity (DID)](#chapter-record-identity))
2. [=Metadata=]: descriptive dimensions of the Record ([Metadata](#chapter-metadata))
3. [=Payload=]: the content of the Record ([Payload and Versions](#chapter-payload))
4. [=Integrity=]: the hash of the Record ([Record Integrity](#chapter-record-integrity))

<pre class='example json' title='Record components'>
{
  "did": "did:rwp:d2d14675-50c9-463d-b564-1ea50644c836:a95437ee-25e4-469a-a2e1-c2b250b21e15",
  "metadata": {},
  "payload": [],
  "recordHash": "sha256:57715def0857e907168e5c74240e6e6d2e8679a66f8da7f1d2e7f09e1cf93b06"
}
</pre>

Any concrete Record is typed by `metadata.core.recordType` and `metadata.core.schemaVersion`, based on a [SchemaRecord](#chapter-schema-record).


## Taxonomy ## {#section-record-taxonomy}

For semantic interoperability, a Record is a specialization of 'prov:Entity'. The term Record remains the primary term of this specification. The term 'prov:Entity' is used only for a [[!PROVO]]-compatible semantic representation.

```text
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rw:   <https://w3id.org/recordweb/ontology#> .
rw:Record
  rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity .
```


## Conceptual Purpose ## {#section-record-purpose}

Every [=Record=] has one primary conceptual purpose (as described in [[RWC]]):
- [=InformationRecord=]
- [=RelationRecord=]
- [=AgentRecord=]
- [=SystemRecord=]

The conceptual purpose MUST NOT be interpreted as a mandatory implementation inheritance hierarchy, a mandatory JSON type hierarchy, or a replacement for concrete Record Types and their SchemaRecords.


